  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nAlthough isolated thunderstorms may be ongoing across northeast\nOklahoma Thursday evening, the best chance of showers and \nthunderstorms during the entire forecast period remains Thursday\nnight and into early Friday, as a disturbance moves through the\nregion. The southward extent of the highest storm chances during\nthis period remains somewhat in question, with the area along and\nnorth of I-40 favored. A low severe weather chance will continue\ninto Thursday night, with perhaps a slightly higher potential for\nlarge hail compared to the afternoon, much like has been observed\nin Kansas with the current activity. The focus for afternoon\nthunderstorms during the day Friday currently looks to be south of\nI-40 east-northeastward into parts of northwest Arkansas, although\nconfidence in this is more on the medium side. The NBM POPs for\nFriday afternoon have been retooled some with this idea in mind,\nkeeping lower POPs to the north across northeast Oklahoma given\nthe confidence. Temperatures on Friday and the potential for\nanother round of dangerous heat will hinge on any residual cloud\ncover and lingering showers/storms through the day. The new NBM\nguidance increased afternoon temperatures from its earlier \nofferings, and these values have been adjusted downward slightly\nusing the short-term blended guidance. Even using this, most areas\nstill have forecast highs a couple of degrees above normal. Into\nFriday night and possibly Saturday, there is an increasing signal\nfor another disturbance to move through far northeast Oklahoma \nand western Arkansas with another round of showers and\nthunderstorms. Once again, the NBM POPs have been retooled Friday\nnight to increase values mainly along and east of the\nOklahoma/Arkansas state line. It is possible that these increased\nPOPs may need to be extended into Saturday on future shifts, but\nfor this forecast, settled on raising them to just below\nmentionable Saturday morning. Thereafter, the much advertised\nreturn to dry conditions and dangerous heat remains on track for\nthe rest of the weekend and into next week. Additional heat\nheadlines will likely be needed during this period.",
